34101.sol:
Let $f(x)=x|x|=x|x|, g(x)=\sin x$ and $h(x)=g o f(x)=g[f(x)] 
\begin{aligned}
&\therefore h(x)= \begin{cases}\sin x^2, & x \geq 0 \\
-\sin x^2, & x < 0\end{cases} \\
&\text{ Now, } h^{\prime}(x)=\left\{$\begin{array}{cc}
2 x $\cos x^2$, & x \geq 0 \\
-2 x $\cos x^2$, & x < 0
\end{array}$\right.
\end{aligned}

Since, L.H.L and $R.H.L atx=0 of h^{\prime}(x)$ is equal to 0 therefore $h^{\prime}(x)$ is continuous at $x=0$
Now, suppose $h^{\prime}(x)$is differentiable

\begin{aligned}
&\therefore h^{\prime \prime}(x) \\
&= \begin{cases}2\left(\cos x^2+2 x^2\left(-\sin x^2\right),\right. & x \geq 0 \\
2\left(-\cos x^2+2 x^2 \sin x^2\right), & x < 0\end{cases}
\end{aligned}

Since, L.H.L$ and R.H.L atx=0 of h^{\prime \prime}(x)$ are different therefore $h^{\prime \prime}(x)$ is not continuous.
$\Rightarrow h^{\prime \prime}(x)$ is not differentiable
$\Rightarrow$ our assumption is wrong
Hence $h^{\prime}(x)$ is not differentiable at $x=0$.$

49457.sol:
Given series, $\frac{1^{2}}{1}+\frac{1^{2}+2^{2}}{1+2}+\frac{1^{2}+2^{2}+3^{2}}{1+2+3}+\ldots$ The nth terms of the series is T_{n}=$\frac{\Sigma n^{2}}{\Sigma n}=\frac{n(n+1)(2 n+1)}{\frac{6 n(n+1)}{2}}=\frac{(2 n+1)}{3}$ Now, $S_{n}=\frac{1}{3}(\Sigma n+\Sigma 1) \begin{aligned}$&=$\frac{1}{3}\left\{2 $\cdot \frac{n(n+1)}{2}+$ n\right\}=$\frac{n}{3}($ n+1+1) \\
&=$\frac{n(n+2)}{3}
\end{aligned}$

